{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfkFNowGM2Mbm6g4j6qUH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VwKEV9U1qLSWqkzZvQoBuu","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"D=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_3\ncat << 'EOF' > $D/hypothesis.md\nRefinement of the incumbent `gamblers_second_order_person_lapse`: people still judge a sequence random to the extent their own second-order, gambler's-fallacy model of a fair coin explains it better than the regular generators they suspect (a switch-biased coin, a biased coin, a repeating short motif with slips, with each person's own motif suspicion), the evidence weighed per flip by a fitted power of length, with person-specific sensitivity and side habit. The single change is to what happens when a person does not follow their judgment: instead of picking a side at random, a person on a person-specific share of trials gives the response mapped the wrong way round — choosing the sequence that looks *less* random (confusing the instruction or the buttons) — and for a few participants this reversal is the rule rather than the exception, so they agree with the majority well below chance on clear-cut pairs, which a random lapse (which can only pull agreement towards one half) cannot produce; this addresses the FDR-surviving critique that participants differ in their rate of agreeing with the majority more than the lapse model produces.\nEOF\necho reversed_mapping_second_order_gamblers > $D/model_name.txt\npython3 - << 'EOF'\nsrc=open('/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/models/gamblers_second_order_person_lapse.py').read()\ni=src.index('import math')\ndoc='''\"\"\"Person-specific response reversals (instead of random lapses) on the gambler's second-order chance coin.\n\nRefinement of `gamblers_second_order_person_lapse`. Single change: the\nperson-specific share f_i of trials on which the participant does not follow\ntheir judgment is no longer a random pick but a reversed response mapping\n(choosing the sequence that looks less random):\np_left = (1 - f_i) * p_judge + f_i * (1 - p_judge), with f_i on a non-centred\nlogit-normal population (400 slots) spanning (0, 1). f_i <= 1/2 reproduces a\nrandom lapse of share 2 f_i; f_i > 1/2 describes a participant who mostly\nanswers the reverse question, agreeing with the majority below chance on\nclear-cut pairs — which a random lapse cannot produce. Everything else\n(second-order and gambler's-fallacy chance coin vs. Markov coin, biased coin\nand repeating motif with person-specific motif suspicion, per-flip length\nscaling, person-specific sensitivity, switch belief and side habit) is the\nincumbent's.\n\"\"\"\n\n'''\nsrc=doc+src[i:]\nold=src[src.index('    # Person-specific lapse share'):src.index('    chose_left = pm.Data')]\nnew='''    # Person-specific reversal share: on those trials the response mapping is\n    # reversed and the less random-looking sequence is chosen.\n    mu_rev = pm.Normal(\"mu_rev\", mu=-3.0, sigma=1.0)\n    sigma_rev = pm.HalfNormal(\"sigma_rev\", sigma=1.5)\n    z_rev = pm.Normal(\"z_rev\", mu=0.0, sigma=1.0, shape=400)\n    rev = pm.math.sigmoid(mu_rev + sigma_rev * z_rev)[pid]\n\n    p_judge = pm.math.sigmoid(eta + side[pid])\n    p_left = pm.Deterministic(\n        \"p_left\", pt.clip((1.0 - rev) * p_judge + rev * (1.0 - p_judge), 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n    )\n\n'''\nsrc=src.replace(old,new)\nopen('/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_3/candidate.py','w').write(src)\nEOF\ntail -25 $D/candidate.py"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1549,"cache_read_input_tokens":83273,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_